 now for the past two hours you\'d say, yeah?\nPatient: Yep.\nDoctor: OK. Did you have any kind of viral illness in the recent past, past couple of weeks?\nPatient: \nPatient: No.\nDoctor: No cold, cough, nothing.\nPatient: No, I\'ve been pretty healthy I think, um, yeah.\nDoctor: Yeah, OK. Um, you\'ve mentioned that you have a, some ear buzzing kind of thing. Any kind of ear pain or ear discharge, anything like that?\nPatient: No, nothing.\nDoctor: OK. Do you have a temperature at all?\nPatient: I don\'t think so, um.\nDoctor: OK, right. You also mentioned that you can walk, but you\'re not walking straight. Why is that? Is it because\nPatient: I think just \'cause the room feels like it\'s moving a bit. Um\nDoctor: Feels like to move . That\'s why you\'re feeling like you\'re a bit unsteady, yeah?\nPatient: I\'ll look .\nPatient: My, my legs and arms and everything are working fine I just, I don\'t feel like I\'m stable. My, the room is spinning.\nDoctor: So there\'s no weakness of your arms and legs. OK.\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: K. Right. Um, so you mentioned you had something like this a month ago. So how, how, how long did that episode last for?\nPatient: Probably only about five, ten minutes.\nDoctor: OK, right. And do you have any problems hearing at the moment? Like, is it muffled or something?\nPatient: No.\nPatient: It\'s just a bit of a buzzy noise in my ears, but otherwise I can hear everything.\nDoctor: OK. Righty-ho.\nDoctor: OK. So, have you been physically sick?\nPatient: No, but now I\'m feeling very sick.\nDoctor: Nausea, OK.\nDoctor: Righty-ho. And, I don\'t have much information of your past medical history. So is there any medical conditions you\'d like to tell me, surgical, medical conditions?\nPatient: Um, I\'ve had premature ovarian failure, um, and I\'ve taken HRT.\nDoctor: OK charting premature hearing failure. OK, and um any other medications at all?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: OK, do you have any allergies at all?\nPatient: No.\nDoctor: \nDoctor: \nDoctor: OK. Right. OK so , talking to you today, um, in the , the most common um, you know, diagnosis we would you\'re suffering from at the moment is something called labyrinthitis. Yeah?\nDoctor: OK, that\'s when your middle ear...there is something um, some inflammation going on there, and there is some imbalances in the crystals there in your middle ear.\nDoctor: OK, so what the most important treatment is actually giving you some kind of middle ear stability so that you won\'t feel as if you\'re going around in a boat, kind of feeling.\nDoctor: OK? So that\'s what we need to . Because to actually make you function a bit more, we\'ll start you on certain medications.\nDoctor: I would still like you to be seen by one of our doctors to take a look inside your ears to just make sure. So this is like just symptomatic relief like you have a fever we just give paracetamol. So just to, you know, make you able to come and see one of the doctors and things like that.\nDoctor: while you\'re with the doctors, we\'d like to do your blood pressure, just examine your, you know, you know, how your walking and everything is.\nDoctor: OK, while we\'re waiting, while you\'re waiting on seeing the doctor if you double up any weakness of your legs or arms or, or, you know, a sudden headache.\nPatient: OK.\nDoctor: Any of those symptoms, or you start being violently sick with it, then you know, you would need to take your to A and E.\nPatient: OK.\nDoctor: But if not, wait for the appointment for the doctor to examine you um and we\'ll take it from there.\nDoctor: So i\'ll, i\'ll send the a prescription at cross pharmacy looks like it\'s the Lloyd pharmacy on our system. Is that correct?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: Yeah. So we\'ll send you across that prescription. Um, it\'s called , yeah? It\'s one tablet three times a day. So I\'ll give you about ten days worth.\nDoctor: In the interim, obviously in the next few days, you\'ll get to see one of my doctors and we\'ll have to just take it from there.\nPatient: OK, thank you so much.\nDoctor: Any questions at all?\nPatient: Um, can you take, do you need to take those tablets with food? Or can you just take them any time?\nDoctor: It doesn\'t really matter, you can take it, because sometimes, you know, you get the symptoms as soon as you wake up, so probably you\'ll feel a bit sick to eat anything so might as well just take the tablets.\nPatient: \nPatient: OK, that\'s great. Thank you so much, that\'s so helpful.\nDoctor: Just make sure you call support to book that appointment, OK?\nPatient: I will, thank you very much.\nDoctor: Bye bye now. Bye\nPatient:\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nSevere dizziness, nausea, and unsteady gait.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient, Mary Jane Grey, is a 45-year-old female who presents via telemedicine for an emergency consultation due to severe dizziness, nausea, and unsteady gait that began approximately 2 hours ago. The symptoms are continuous and have significantly impacted her ability to walk straight and perform daily tasks. She also reports a mild buzzing in her ears. The patient mentions a similar episode one month ago, which resolved quickly, but this episode has persisted for a longer duration. She denies any recent viral illness or other systemic symptoms.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **General:** Reports feeling very sick but denies fever.\n- **Ears:** Mild buzzing but no pain or discharge.\n- **Neurological:** Severe dizziness and unsteady gait.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Nausea present.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** No weakness in arms or legs.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n- **Visual:** Patient’s face not fully visible due to poor video quality.\n- **Vital Signs:** Not available (telemedicine consultation).\n- **Physical Examination:** Not conducted (telemedicine consultation).\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Diagnosis:** Other labyrinthine diseases (ICD-10 H81.4).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Treatment:**\n  - Prescribed Meclizine 25 mg, 1 tablet, 3 times a day orally for symptomatic relief of dizziness.\n- **Follow-up:**\n  - Patient to follow up with a specialist ENT doctor within the next few days for further examination.\n- **Referral:**\n  - Referral to Dr. John Smith, ENT specialist, for further evaluation and management of severe dizziness and unsteady gait.\n- **Investigations:**\n  - Blood pressure examination to be done during the follow-up appointment.\n  - Awaiting results of ear examination by the specialist to rule out any infection or other abnormalities.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient was advised to take the prescribed medication as directed and to wait for the follow-up appointment with the ENT specialist.\n- If symptoms worsen or new symptoms arise, the patient was instructed to go to the nearest Accident and Emergency (A&E) department.\n- The patient was given a prescription for Meclizine and instructed to go to Lloyd pharmacy for collection.\n- The patient was also informed to call support services to book the follow-up appointment with the ENT specialist. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ History : \nHx of dizziness for 2 hrs \nDizziness is spinning room around her \nNausea and vomiting \nBuzzing in ears \nNot able to walk properly \nNo ear pain \nNo hearing problems \nNo fever \nHx of similar episode 1 month back \nHx of premature ovarian failure \nNo other medical or surgical history \nNo allergies \nNo other medications \nNo smoking \nNo alcohol \nNo illicit drug use \nNo travel history \nNo viral illness \nNo headaches \nNo weakness \nNo chest pain \nNo abdominal pain \nNo back pain \nNo rash \nNo itching \nNo cough \nNo shortness of breath \nNo palpitation \nNo numbness \nNo tingling \nNo falls \nNo faints \nNo swelling \nNo discharge \nNo pain \nNo skin problems \nNo bleeding \nNo discharge \nNo leg pain \nNo swelling \nNo chest pain \nNo palpitations \nNo shortness of breath \nNo cold \nNo cough \nN