octor: sexually active at the moment?\nPatient: Yes, yeah.\nDoctor: And, um, .\nPatient: You mentioned about periods. I did notice a bit of, um.\nPatient: I don\'t know it\'s like , not necessarily like spotting, but in like, in my, my pee is like a little, I suppose. I think I thought it was my period, it\'s like a little bit, pink.\nDoctor: In your pee, did you say? Your, so your pee is slightly red? Or slightly pink?\nPatient: \nPatient: But I am kind of like due, on my, didn\'t know if that was like, yeah, a part of it.\nDoctor: OK. So, when was your last menstrual period?\nPatient: Um, around a month ago, yeah, three and a, three and a bit weeks ago.\nDoctor: Ah, OK. Um, so it\'s three .\nDoctor: And, uh, did you say? So you\'re sexually active, you\'re using contraception? And what?\nPatient: Mm-hmm, yeah. I\'ve got the arm, the implants.\nDoctor: Um.\nDoctor: And.\nDoctor: Uh, , one sexual partner? Um, a regular, regular boyfriend or?\nPatient: Yeah.\nPatient: Yep.\nDoctor: Um.\nDoctor: , to go back to your symptom of, uh, constipation. Can I just clarify, when was the last time you had a bowel motion?\nPatient: Uh, yesterday.\nDoctor: OK, and, uh, do you feel like, you, are still quite bunged up at the moment?\nPatient: Uh, no it seems to have cleared up. So I was, about a week ago, but I\'m OK, OK now, the last few days.\nPatient: Yeah, I K.\nDoctor: OK, all right. So you, just to sort of confirm your symptoms. Um, you\'re feeling sort of sick, nauseous, with tummy pain, which is fairly constant.\nDoctor: Um, how, , did the tummy pain come on suddenly or gradually?\nPatient: Um, it\'s only been for the last two days. Um, so I guess yeah, quite recently.\nDoctor: OK.\nDoctor: So it\'s, it\'s come on gradually, has it?\nPatient: Yeah.\nDoctor: Not a sudden onset?\nDoctor: OK, and uh, your last menstrual period was just three and a bit weeks ago. Um, and you, but you say you\'ve had some . A little bit of spotting, possibly before um, in-between periods, or re