) and refer to a urologist if symptoms persist.\n- Order a urine pregnancy test to rule out pregnancy.\n- Discuss the importance of maintaining a balanced diet and increasing fluid intake.\n- Advise the patient to avoid Amoxicillin due to her allergy.\n- Provide information on managing nausea and suggest over-the-counter antacids if necessary.\n- Educate the patient on recognizing signs of worsening symptoms and when to seek urgent medical attention.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient will follow up with a GP for further examination and investigation.\n- She is advised to return to the urgent care center if her symptoms worsen or if she experiences any new symptoms. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ History : \n1. Nausea and vomiting\n2. Lower abdominal pain\n3. Increased frequency of micturition\n4. Lower abdominal ache below the umbilicus\n5. Constipation for 1 week, now resolved\n6. No diarrhea\n7. No pain while passing urine\n8. No dysuria\n9. No hematuria\n10. No frequency of passing urine\n11. No discharge\n12. No spotting of blood in the urine\n13. Last menstrual period was 3 weeks ago\n14. No pregnancy\n15. Allergic to Amoxicillin\n16. No other medical problems\n17. No other drug allergies\n18. No regular medications\n19. No smoking\n20. Lives with her partner\n\nPlan : \n1. Further history and examination\n2. R/O pregnancy\n3. R/O UTI\n4. R/O Gastroenteritis\n5. R/O pregnancy\n6. R/O pelvic infection\n7. R/O pelvic inflammatory disease\n8. R/O sexually transmitted diseases\n9. R/O ovarian cyst\n10. R/O appendicitis\n11. R/O pelvic inflammatory disease\n12. R/O pelvic infection\n13. R/O pelvic abscess\n14. R/O ectopic pregnancy\n15. R/O miscarriage\n16. R/O ovarian torsion\n17. R/O ovarian cyst\n18. R/O ovarian cyst\n19. R/O pelvic mass\n20. R/O pelvic inflammatory disease\n\nDiagnosis : \n1. Gastroenteritis\n2. Constipation\n3. Nausea and vomiting\n4. Lower abdominal ache\n5. UTI\n6. Pregnancy\n7. Pelvic infection\n8. Pelvic inflammatory disease\n9. Ovari