-year-old Caucasian female presenting with a moderate throbbing headache that has persisted for 1 day. The headache is associated with sensitivity to light and mild photophobia. The patient reports that the pain has been continuous since its onset and has significantly impacted her daily activities, leaving her unable to leave the house. She denies any nausea or vomiting. The headache is described as throbbing and localized to the left side of the head. She has not had any similar headaches in the past and does not have a known history of migraines.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **General:** No fever or chills.\n- **Eyes:** Sensitivity to light, mild photophobia.\n- **Head:** Throbbing headache, moderate in severity.\n- **Neurological:** No weakness, tingling, or numbness.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** No nausea or vomiting.\n- **Respiratory:** No cough or shortness of breath.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** No joint pain or muscle aches.\n- **Skin:** No rashes or changes in skin texture.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Not taken during this visit.\n- Temperature: Not taken during this visit.\n- Heart Rate: Not taken during this visit.\n- Respiratory Rate: Not taken during this visit.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General Appearance:** Alert, cooperative, and in no acute distress.\n- **Head:** No visible signs of trauma or abnormalities.\n- **Eyes:** Pupils equal, round, and reactive to light.\n- **Neck:** Supple, no lymphadenopathy.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no organomegaly.\n- **Neurological:** Alert and oriented, no focal deficits.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Headache, unspecified.\n- **Differential Diagnosis:** Migraine, tension headache, tension-type headache.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medication:** \n  - Ibuprofen 400 mg, oral, every 6 hours as needed for pain, 30 tablets.\n  - Paracetamol 500 mg, oral, every 6 hours as needed for pain, 20 tablets.\n- **Follow-up Recommendations:** \n  - Recheck in 3 days if symptoms persist.\n- **Lifestyle Change:**\n  - Increase hydration.\n- **Referral to Specialists:**\n  - Referral to Dr. Sarah Thompson, Neurologist, for further evaluation if headaches persist or worsen.\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:**\n  - Discussed the importance of adequate hydration and avoiding known triggers for headaches.\n  - Advised to monitor headache severity and any associated symptoms.\n  - Educated on the potential side effects of prescribed medications and the importance of adherence to the medication regimen.\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. She was advised to follow up immediately if symptoms worsen or if new symptoms arise. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Hx: \nHx of 1 day of throbbing headache on left side of head. \nPain worse with light. \nPain worse when lying flat. \nPain better when lying down. \nNo visual changes. No vomiting or nausea. \nNo tingling/weakness in limbs. \nNo fevers. \nNo change in BM. \nNo other sx. \nPMH: nil\nDH: microgynon\nFH: mum - high BP, migraines\nSH: lives with housemates. Drinks alcohol socially. No smoking, drug use. \nImp: migraine. \nPlan: anti-inflammatories + paracetamol. Review in 24-48hrs if no improvement. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nLeft sided headache since yesterday morning. Intermittent. Non positional. Not woken her up. No altered smell/taste.  E&D normally. No prodromal illness, no viral symptoms, sore ears/coryzal sx.  Doesn\'t feel feverish but hasn\'t checked temperature. No N&V. No tingling/numbness/weakness/rash. Worse with light but vision not altered. Taken paracetamol only. Period stopped last week.\nNo hx headaches.\nPMH: Nil significant\nDH: Microgynon - no SEs, BP was normal when checked for last prescription ? when.\nFH: Mother has hypertension and migraines.\nSH: Lives with flatmates, alcohol - few glasses wine at weekends, non smoker.\nSome stress at work recently. \nImp: ? Tension headache/first episode migraine. No features meningitic illness but is worse with light.\n Plan: Advised to get BP and temp checked and to call straight back if worsening/persistent headache/fever/rash/new symptoms. Advised to start regular ibuprofen with food. To have a review if regular headaches.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
