y much.\nPatient: Yep, bye.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, a 35-year-old male, presents with severe headaches, hot flashes, reduced appetite, and occasional tingling in the extremities. \n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports experiencing these symptoms intermittently, particularly during work hours, which have significantly impacted his ability to concentrate and perform his job duties as a lawyer in London. The symptoms have been ongoing for a few months since he changed jobs. He describes the severity of the headaches as severe, and they are associated with occasional nausea and a feeling of shortness of breath. \n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports feeling hot, reduced appetite, unintentional weight loss of 5 lbs.\n- **Neurological:** Occasional tingling in the extremities, severe headaches.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Feeling of shortness of breath.\n- **Psychiatric:** Reports stress levels at a 7/10, significant stress at work.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Heart Rate: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Respiratory Rate: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Temperature: Not measured during this consultation.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Patient appears anxious but cooperative.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Respiratory:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Neurological:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Endocrine:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Disorder of the brain, unspecified (ICD-10: R29.9).\n- **Secondary Diagnosis:** Hyperthyroidism (pre-existing condition).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Follow-up Recommendations:** \n  - The patient is advised to follow up in the clinic for a comprehensive evaluation by an endocrinologist.\n- **Referral:**\n  - Referral to Dr. Emily Carter, an endocrinologist, for evaluation of hyperthyroidism and its impact on current symptoms.\n- **Blood Work:**\n  - Order thyroid function tests (TSH, Free T4) to monitor hyperthyroidism.\n- **Lifestyle Change:**\n  - Advise reducing alcohol consumption from 2 glasses of wine per week to once a month.\n- **Medication Adjustment:**\n  - No medications prescribed at this time; will be reconsidered after specialist evaluation.\n- **Imaging:**\n  - MRI of the brain to rule out any structural abnormalities.\n\nThe patient was counseled about the potential causes of his symptoms and the importance of follow-up and specialist consultation. He was reassured and given a detailed explanation of the next steps in his management plan. The patient expressed understanding and agreed to the proposed plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ 1. General\nThe patient is a 35-year-old male, working as a lawyer in London. He has noticed a change in his health since changing his job. He feels hot, severe headaches, reduced appetite, tingling in his arms, palpitations and has lost 5lbs unintentionally.\n2. Headaches\nHeadaches are present when he is working on his computer. They are not at a specific time of the day. They are in the front of his head. They are associated with nausea. He is not sick. They are 7/10. He is not sure if they are getting worse. They are not relieved with paracetamol. They are affecting his work. He is not taking any medications. There is no neck stiffness.\n3. Reduced appetite\nHe has reduced appetite. He has lost 5lbs unintentionally. He is not exercising. He is not feeling looser in his clothes. No one has commented on his weight loss. He is eating out.\n4. Tingling in the arms\nThe tingling is in both hands. It is more in the morning. It is when he is in the lift. It is not present in his legs. It is a pins and needles type sensation.\n5. Shortness of breath\nHe feels short of breath. He is not sure if it is related to his heart rate. It is not when he is exercising. It is when he is sitting at his desk. It is not at night. It is not related to his thyroid. He is not on any medications.\n6. Palpitations\nHe feels his heart beating. It is not when he is exercising. It is not at a specific time of the day. It is once a day. It is not associated with headaches. It is not related to his thyroid. He is not on any medications.\n7. Loss of libido\nHe has noticed a loss of libido at home.\n8. Past medical history\nHe has hyperthyroidism. He is on thyroxine. He is not allergic to anything. He is a smoker. He drinks alcohol once a week. He does not take recreational drugs. He is not on any medications.\n9. Examination\nHe is a well-looking man. He is not in distress. He is not pale. He is not dehydrated. He has good peripheral perfusion. He is not tachycardic. He has a regular rhythm. He has no murmurs. He has good air entry bilaterally. He has no crepitations. He has no }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nPC. Headaches, loss of appetite, tingling, feel hot, palpitations,  acute onset, at random times of the day while at work (lawyer)\n\nHPC: \nPC started when started new job (lawer, high stress, works in front of a computer)\nHeadaches:\n-\tNo pre aura, \n-\tFrontal\n-\tNo radiation\n-\tSome nausea  no vomiting,\n-\tNo pmhx of headache.\n\nReduced appetite, with unintentional weight loss, loss of 5lbs \nPins and needles in both arms, no radiation to legs, maintains motor and sense, no loss of function\nFevers with headaches \nPalpitations with mild SOBOE and SOBAR.\n\n\nSymptoms occur at random times of the day while at work, with no pre-symptoms, sometimes together, sometimes individually.\n\nPMHx , hypothyroid. \n\nDHx allergies - nil\nThyroxine. \n\nSHx lawer , stressful job.\n\nDDx ??hypothyroid?\n\nPlans : requires further examination and blood tests.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include