y much.\nPatient: Yep, bye.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nThe patient, a 35-year-old male, presents with severe headaches, hot flashes, reduced appetite, and occasional tingling in the extremities. \n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient reports experiencing these symptoms intermittently, particularly during work hours, which have significantly impacted his ability to concentrate and perform his job duties as a lawyer in London. The symptoms have been ongoing for a few months since he changed jobs. He describes the severity of the headaches as severe, and they are associated with occasional nausea and a feeling of shortness of breath. \n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports feeling hot, reduced appetite, unintentional weight loss of 5 lbs.\n- **Neurological:** Occasional tingling in the extremities, severe headaches.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Feeling of shortness of breath.\n- **Psychiatric:** Reports stress levels at a 7/10, significant stress at work.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Heart Rate: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Respiratory Rate: Not measured during this consultation.\n- Temperature: Not measured during this consultation.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Patient appears anxious but cooperative.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Respiratory:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Neurological:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n- **Endocrine:** Not examined in detail during this consultation.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Disorder of the brain, unspecified (ICD-10: R29.9).\n- **Secondary Diagnosis:** Hyperthyroidism (pre-existing condition).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Follow-up Recommendations:** \n  - The patient is advised to follow up in the clinic for