se, sir?\nPatient: Uh, I try and walk to the shops, at least once a day.\nDoctor: OK.\nPatient: Um, but uh, that\'s about it, I\'ve. Yeah, I don\'t, I don\'t go running like I used to when i was young.\nDoctor: OK.\nDoctor: Right, OK. And certainly now your symptoms, you, you\'re, you said you\'re feeling more breathless even walking to the uh, to the shop, she said.\nDoctor: Um, right. Ohh um, based on your, based on you history, and having listened to your story, I, I wonder whether you\'ve had a flare-up of your heart failure, sir. Um,\nDoctor: Which can sometimes happen. Um, um, and uh,\nPatient: Mmm.\nPatient: That.\nDoctor: It\'s . So, it seems like your heart\'s not pumping as well as it should be. Um, and obviously having diagnosed with heart failure, there\'s obviously\nPatient: What\'s going on.\nDoctor: Um, that\'s the first thing that comes to my mind. Um, now there\'s a couple of options move forward. Um,\nPatient: Mmm.\nDoctor: Given that you\'re otherwise well at the moment, and having examined you, your chest, and your saturations and blood pressure are all OK,\nDoctor: I\'m quite happy to increase your dose of Furosemide. Maybe, rather than one tablet a day, we can do two tablets a day, for the first few days.\nDoctor: Um, and see, and see how you\'re getting on.\nPatient: OK. If you think that\'ll be better.\nDoctor: Uh,\nDoctor: I think, I think, it\'s, it\'s, it\'s definitely worth treating your symptoms. But what we should also think about is maybe doing a couple of other tests, like a blood test, and maybe an echocardiogram of your heart, to see how well your heart\'s pumping.\nPatient: OK.\nDoctor: Is that something you\'ve had before? Yeah, so um, an , it\'s a, it\'s a general scan of your heart. You might have had this done before, when you were diagnosed. And it\'s just to get a good\nPatient: What does that?\nPatient: Ohh yes, I remember now.\nDoctor: Sorry?\nPatient: Uh, yeah, I remember now. .\nDoctor: You remember that. OK, fine. That\'s something I can organize for you today. And, and that might take a week or two weeks for that to be organized. But, I think certainly in the meantime, let\'s, um,\nDoctor: Let\'s, let\'s get a blood test done. We can check your um, you know your full blood count, look for signs of anemia. We can have a check of your kidney function.\nDoctor: Um, and we can definitely increase your um, your Furosemide, uh, to you know, one tablet twice a day.\nDoctor: But I really like, would like you to come back and see me in about a week\'s time, see how you\'re getting on. That sound reasonable?\nPatient: I think that sounds like a good plan.\nDoctor: And of course, in the meantime if you are getting more unwell, feel like you\'re getting more breathless, or you\'re having any other symptoms like chest pain, or palpitations or dizziness, then, uh, you know I want you to give me a call sooner than that, and we can have a chat on the phone as well.\nPatient: OK. I\'ll see how I get on.\nDoctor: OK? Do you have any questions for me?\nPatient: Uh, I think that\'s all pretty clear. Thank you very much Doctor.\nDoctor: Great, and then well I wish you all the best! Have a good day. OK. Thank you.\nPatient: OK. See you soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nShortness of breath.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nOscar Johnson is a 60-year-old African American male who presents via telemedicine for an initial consultation due to worsening shortness of breath over the past 2 weeks, with a notable exacerbation in the past 2 days. The patient reports mild to moderate shortness of breath, which has progressively worsened, now requiring minimal exertion such as walking to the shops, necessitating frequent rest periods. He also experiences occasional dry cough and mild swelling in the ankles. The patient is concerned about his worsening symptoms and their potential impact on his daily activities and work.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports feeling generally run-down but no fever or weight loss.\n- **Respiratory:** Shortness of breath, occasional dry cough.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Mild ankle swelling, no chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** No nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n- **Genitourinary:** No dysuria or hematuria.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Mild swelling in ankles, no joint pain.\n- **Neurological:** No dizziness, headaches, or syncope.\n- **Psychiatric:** Reports feeling anxious about his health condition.\n\n**Past Medical History (PMH):**\n- Heart failure, diagnosed 5 years ago.\n- Myocardial infarction, history of.\n- Hypertension, history of.\n\n**Family History:**\n- Father had a myocardial infarction at age 60.\n- Mother had osteoporosis.\n\n**Social History:**\n- Retired, lives on a fixed income.\n- Works part-time as a lorry driver.\n- Former smoker, quit 10 years ago.\n- Does not consume alcohol.\n- Enjoys watching TV and reading books.\n- Limited exercise, walks to the shops daily.\n- Lives in a small city with moderate pollution.\n- Semi-impoverished neighborhood, limited access to healthy food options.\n\n**Current Medications:**\n- Diuretics (Furosemide 40 mg once daily).\n- Aspirin 75 mg once daily.\n- Metoprolol 50 mg once daily.\n\n**Allergies:**\n- None.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: Not taken during this telemedicine session.\n- Heart Rate: Not taken during this telemedicine session.\n- Respiratory Rate: Not taken during this telemedicine session.\n- Temperature: Not taken during this telemedicine session.\n- Oxygen Saturation: Not taken during this telemedicine session.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Appears slightly anxious, cooperative, and well-nourished.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear breath sounds, no wheezes or rales.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n- **Extremities:** Mild ankle swelling, no cyanosis or clubbing.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Heart failure exacerbation.\n- Hypertension.\n- History of myocardial infarction.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medication Adjustment:** Increase Furosemide to 80 mg once daily for 3 days.\n- **Follow-up:** Schedule follow-up appointment in 1 week.\n- **Referral to Tests:** \n  - Blood work: CBC, BMP, LFTs.\n  - Echocardiogram to assess cardiac function.\n- **Imaging:** Echocardiogram to evaluate cardiac function.\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. He is advised to monitor his symptoms and report any worsening of shortness of breath, chest pain, or other concerning symptoms immediately. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ 1.2. Hx of NYHA class 2 HF with diuretic\n2.2/52 hx of progressive SOB, worse in last 2/7\nSOB on minimal exertion, cough, ankle oedema\nNo chest pain, palpitations, fevers, lethargy, loss of appetite, weight gain\nNo recent change in diet, no smoking, no EtOH\nDH: Furosemide 40mg od, Bisoprolol 1.25mg od, Aspirin 75mg od\nFMH: Father – MI (s/o), Mother – osteoporosis\nSH: Lives alone, works part-time as lorry driver, 2 cats (Polly and Tim)\nPMH: MI 5/2015, diagnosed with HF 6/2015\nImp: Acute decompensated HF\nPlan:\n1. Increase Furosemide to 2 x 40mg for 3/7 then review\n2. Arrange blood tests (FBC, U&Es, liver function) and echocardiogram\n3. Review in 1 week }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n2/5 hx of SOB, worsening over the past 2/7. Feeling more SOB on minimal exertion, nil SOB at rest. No chest pain/palpitations. Assoc cough - mainly dry. No fevers. Has also noticed ankle odema bilaterally over past few days extending to shins. \nDiagnosed with heart failure previously and currently on diuretics. \nUrinary - Polyuria, nil else\n\nPMH: Heart failure, ?unstable angina \nDH: furosemide, aspirin, bisoprolol, simvastatin\nNKDA\nFH - father died of MI early 60s , mother - osteoporosis \nSH: lives alone, 2 cats , works part time as a lorry driver. Ex-smoker - a pack/d for 25 years. No etOH\n\nImp: exacerbation of heart failure\n\nPlan:\n1.\tIncrease furosemide BD\n2.\tBlood tests, inc U+Es, FBC, BNP\n3.\tArrange echo \n4.\tReview in 7d, or earlier if worsening sx (chest pain, SOB, dizziness, etc)\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


