ent on recognizing symptoms of DVT and PE, and the importance of seeking immediate medical attention if symptoms worsen.\n- Discussed the potential risks associated with her current medications, especially Codeine, given her allergy to Ibuprofen.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- The patient will follow up with her primary care physician after seeing the cardiologist. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ History : \n1. SOB for 1 hour \n2. C-section 4 weeks ago\n3. SOB while watching TV\n4. SOB worse on deep inspiration\n5. Chest soreness\n6. C-section scar sore\n7. SOB not getting worse\n8. No chest pain\n9. No SOB while walking\n10. No SOB at rest\n11. No cough\n12. No sputum production\n13. No haemoptysis\n14. No wheeze\n15. No chest pain\n16. No fever\n17. No palpable crepitations\n18. No palpable lymph nodes\n19. No rashes\n20. No diarrhea\n21. No vomiting\n22. No recent travel\n23. No recent sick contacts\n24. No pets\n25. No smoking\n26. No alcohol\n27. No drug history\n28. No known allergies\n29. no family history of heart problems\n30. no family history of asthma\n31. no family history of diabetes\n32. no family history of hypertension\n33. no family history of mental health problems\n34. no family history of thyroid problems\n35. no family history of epilepsy\n36. no family history of diabetes\n37. no family history of heart problems\n38. no family history of blood clots\n39. no family history of stroke\n40. no family history of thrombosis\n41. no family history of pulmonary embolism\n42. no family history of pulmonary embolism\n43. no family history of blood clots\n44. no family history of stroke\n45. no family history of thrombosis\n46. no family history of pulmonary embolism\n47. no family history of pulmonary embolism\n48. no family history of blood clots\n49. no family history of stroke\n50. no family history of thrombosis }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nPC SOBAR, c section 2-3 weeks ago\n\nHPC;\nSOBAR while watching tv, acute onset.  Cough 1/7 previously, unpr